Disable mutation of configuration resources (minimal #6 approach)

This patch locks configuration files (settings/keybindings/tasks/mcp/extensions)
as read-only with the smallest possible footprint. Two interception points:

1. FileService.writeFile: rejects writes to any file whose name matches
   settings.json / keybindings.json / tasks.json / mcp.json / extensions.json.
   All file writes (editor save, programmatic create, extension writes) go
   through writeFile, so this is the key backstop covering all 5 resource
   types across all ConfigurationTargets (USER_LOCAL/REMOTE/WORKSPACE/FOLDER).

2. WorkspaceService.updateValue: early-returns when configLocked is true,
   blocking all programmatic configuration changes including MEMORY target
   (which bypasses the file system and updates _configuration in-memory
   directly). Uses a class property (not a const) so TypeScript's
   allowUnreachableCode:false does not flag the remaining method body.

What this patch does NOT do (intentionally, for minimal footprint):
  - Does not disable file change listeners (external disk writes via echo
    will still be detected by watchers and reloaded into memory, but the
    file itself cannot be overwritten through the editor or API).
  - Does not disable the Settings UI (users can still open Settings, but
    saving will fail with FILE_PERMISSION_DENIED).
  - Does not register resources dynamically (uses filename matching instead
    of registerReadonlyResource, so no interface changes needed).

Trade-off: filename matching may also block project-level .vscode/settings.json
or .vscode/tasks.json. This is acceptable because those are also configuration
files that should be read-only under this policy.

To verify:

1. Apply: quilt push disable-config-mutation.diff
2. Build: cd lib/vscode && npm install && cd .. && npm run watch
3. Manual: open Settings (Ctrl+,), edit a value, save -> FILE_PERMISSION_DENIED

Relationship with other patches:
  - Independent of all other patches. Can be applied in any order.

